C = [
("h1", "4. Signs to a Nation, a King and the Nations Around Them"),
("p", "Claim: the signs of the Old Testament are given to a people and to its kings, and they carry a test. The signs in Egypt and in the wilderness are given to Egypt and to Israel; the signs at Isaiah’s time are given to the house of David; the promise of Joel is given to Zion. Moses also lays down a rule that a sign alone does not authenticate a prophet. The volume reads the passages in the order the canon places them."),
("sub", "4.1 Egypt, and the signs that were told to the children"),
("p", "Before the plagues the LORD says of Pharaoh, “And I will harden Pharaoh’s heart, and multiply my signs and my wonders in the land of Egypt” (Exodus 7:3). The purpose follows: “And the Egyptians shall know that I am the LORD, when I stretch forth mine hand upon Egypt, and bring out the children of Israel from among them” (Exodus 7:5). The signs are given in the land of Egypt, to Pharaoh and to the Egyptians, and their stated end is that Egypt shall know the LORD."),
("p", "A second purpose is stated before the plague of locusts: “for I have hardened his heart, and the heart of his servants, that I might shew these my signs before him: and that thou mayest tell in the ears of thy son, and of thy son’s son, what things I have wrought in Egypt, and my signs which I have done among them; that ye may know how that I am the LORD” (Exodus 10:1, 2). Here the signs are also for Israel, and for Israel’s children. They are to be told. Moses later asks the people to recall the same events: “Or hath God assayed to go and take him a nation from the midst of another nation, by temptations, by signs, and by wonders, and by war, and by a mighty hand, and by a stretched out arm, and by great terrors, according to all that the LORD your God did for you in Egypt before your eyes?” (Deuteronomy 4:34). The signs are part of the history of a nation, recited as grounds for the nation’s obedience."),
("sub", "4.2 The wilderness, and the signs that did not persuade"),
("p", "The signs do not bring the generation out of Egypt to belief. The LORD says at Kadesh, “How long will this people provoke me? and how long will it be ere they believe me, for all the signs which I have shewed among them?” (Numbers 14:11). He repeats the charge in judgment: “Because all those men which have seen my glory, and my miracles, which I did in Egypt and in the wilderness, and have tempted me now these ten times, and have not hearkened to my voice” (Numbers 14:22). The passage joins three things, seeing, signs and refusal. The generation that saw the miracles is the generation that is judged."),
("p", "The wilderness account therefore carries the same lesson as the Gospels and the lesson of the Old Testament as a whole: a sign is given to a hearer, and the hearer can refuse it. The sign confirms the word; the word still asks to be believed and obeyed."),
("sub", "4.3 The sign that comes to pass, and the prophet who must die"),
("p", "Moses states a rule for the day when signs are used against the LORD. “If there arise among you a prophet, or a dreamer of dreams, and giveth thee a sign or a wonder, and the sign or the wonder come to pass, whereof he spake unto thee, saying, Let us go after other gods, which thou hast not known, and let us serve them; Thou shalt not hearken unto the words of that prophet, or that dreamer of dreams: for the LORD your God proveth you, to know whether ye love the LORD your God with all your heart and with all your soul” (Deuteronomy 13:1–3). The passage allows that the sign or wonder “come to pass.” It does not allow the prophet to be believed. The test is the word: what does the prophet say about the LORD? The penalty is stated in the same chapter: “And that prophet, or that dreamer of dreams, shall be put to death” (Deuteronomy 13:5)."),
("p", "The passage sets a limit on every sign in the volume. A sign confirms a message, and a message that turns the people from the LORD is not confirmed by it. The signs of Moses, Elijah and the Lord are signs given with a message the law and the prophets already recognise. The volume does not reason from the power of a sign to the truth of a message. It reasons from the message the sign accompanied."),
("sub", "4.4 Ahaz, Hezekiah and the house of David"),
("p", "The LORD offers Ahaz a sign: “Ask thee a sign of the LORD thy God; ask it either in the depth, or in the height above” (Isaiah 7:11). Ahaz declines, “I will not ask, neither will I tempt the LORD” (Isaiah 7:12), and the prophet answers him as the house: “Hear ye now, O house of David; Is it a small thing for you to weary men, but will ye weary my God also? Therefore the Lord himself shall give you a sign; Behold, a virgin shall conceive, and bear a son, and shall call his name Immanuel” (Isaiah 7:13, 14). The sign is offered to a king of the house of David, refused, and then given by the Lord “himself” to “you,” the house. Matthew 1:23 takes up the words of Isaiah 7:14 in the Gospel; this volume leaves that reading to the places that treat it."),
("p", "Hezekiah receives a sign at his own request. The word of the LORD is, “And this shall be a sign unto thee from the LORD, that the LORD will do this thing that he hath spoken; Behold, I will bring again the shadow of the degrees, which is gone down in the sun dial of Ahaz, ten degrees backward. So the sun returned ten degrees, by which degrees it was gone down” (Isaiah 38:7, 8). The king had asked, “What is the sign that I shall go up to the house of the LORD?” (Isaiah 38:22). The sign confirms a word of healing and of deliverance to the king of Judah. The recipient is a king of David’s line in Jerusalem."),
("sub", "4.5 Joel, and wonders before the day"),
("p", "Joel promises signs to Zion: “And I will shew wonders in the heavens and in the earth, blood, and fire, and pillars of smoke. The sun shall be turned into darkness, and the moon into blood, before the great and the terrible day of the LORD come. And it shall come to pass, that whosoever shall call on the name of the LORD shall be delivered: for in mount Zion and in Jerusalem shall be deliverance, as the LORD hath said, and in the remnant whom the LORD shall call” (Joel 2:30–32). The wonders are placed “before” the day of the LORD, and the deliverance is placed “in mount Zion and in Jerusalem” and “in the remnant.” The passage is addressed to Israel and concerns its day."),
("p", "Peter quotes the passage at Pentecost to the men of Israel, and Acts 2:19–22 is read in chapter 7. At this point the volume records only that the prophet’s promise of wonders is promised to Zion and to the remnant, and that it is tied to the day of the LORD."),
("sub", "4.6 The nations that meet Israel"),
("p", "Two accounts show what the nations say when they meet Israel’s signs. Rahab of Jericho tells the spies, “I know that the LORD hath given you the land,” and gives her reason: “For we have heard how the LORD dried up the water of the Red sea for you, when ye came out of Egypt; and what ye did unto the two kings of the Amorites, that were on the other side Jordan, Sihon and Og, whom ye utterly destroyed. And as soon as we had heard these things, our hearts did melt, neither did there remain any more courage in any man, because of you: for the LORD your God, he is God in heaven above, and in earth beneath” (Joshua 2:9–11). The signs in Egypt, forty years before, are heard of in Jericho, and Rahab states the conclusion the LORD had stated at the plagues: the LORD is God."),
("p", "Naaman, the captain of the host of the king of Syria, comes to Israel for healing. Elisha sends word to the king of Israel: “let him come now to me, and he shall know that there is a prophet in Israel” (2 Kings 5:8). Naaman dips in the Jordan seven times “according to the saying of the man of God: and his flesh came again like unto the flesh of a little child, and he was clean” (2 Kings 5:14). His answer is the same as Rahab’s: “Behold, now I know that there is no God in all the earth, but in Israel” (2 Kings 5:15). The sign is done by Israel’s prophet, in Israel, and the Gentile who receives it confesses the God of Israel. In both accounts the nations come to the signs by coming to Israel."),
("ctbl", "Signs to a nation, a king and the nations.", [
    ("Passage", "Given to", "Stated purpose or limit"),
    ("Exodus 7:3–5", "Pharaoh and Egypt", "“And the Egyptians shall know that I am the LORD”"),
    ("Exodus 10:1, 2", "Pharaoh; Israel’s children told", "“that ye may know how that I am the LORD”"),
    ("Numbers 14:11, 22", "Israel in the wilderness", "The signs seen; the people did not believe"),
    ("Deuteronomy 13:1–5", "Israel, tested", "A sign that comes to pass does not authenticate a message that turns from the LORD"),
    ("Isaiah 7:11–14", "Ahaz and the house of David", "A sign offered, refused, then given by the Lord himself"),
    ("Isaiah 38:7, 8, 22", "Hezekiah", "That the LORD will do what he has spoken"),
    ("Joel 2:30–32", "Zion and the remnant", "Wonders before the day of the LORD"),
    ("Joshua 2:9–11; 2 Kings 5:8, 15", "Rahab; Naaman", "The nations know the LORD by coming to Israel and its prophet"),
  ], [0.24, 0.30, 0.46]),
("sub", "What the volume takes from the passages"),
("bul", [
    "The signs in Egypt are given to Egypt that it may know the LORD and to Israel that its children may be told.",
    "The signs in the wilderness are seen and refused; a sign gives no guarantee of belief.",
    "Moses states that a sign which comes to pass does not authenticate a message that turns Israel from the LORD.",
    "Signs are offered and given to kings of David’s line and are promised to Zion and the remnant.",
    "The Gentiles who confess the LORD on account of the signs, Rahab and Naaman, confess Him by coming to Israel.",
    "No passage in this chapter is addressed to anyone outside Israel’s line.",
  ]),
]
